tion one you might not know you  miss until you don't have it anymore  in a study performed after the 2003 SARS  outbreak individuals who were  quarantined often had high levels of  psychological distress this was not just  from isolation but also a feeling of  uncertainty from a lack of clear or  reliable information a nationwide  lockdown of more than 330 million people  would have huge mental and economic  consequences but is it the best way to  stop a virus pandemic well it does slow  it down  we know that slowing the spread of a  disease means that hospitals will be  better equipped to handle patients  urgent needs and save lives but a  mandatory geographic quarantine is the  most severe form of a lockdown and would  probably be seen as unconstitutional in  the u.s. instead the US federal  government typically provides support  funding and expert opinions while  leaving city and state officials to  decide on specific measures closing  schools banning bars and restaurants  canceling all events and urging people  to work from home were all mandates that  happened during the pandemic in 2020 in  times of pandemics and lockdowns  we have to remember our individual  actions and decisions really do matter  and make a difference most important we  are all part of a community and we need  to unite together even if we can't  physically be together  one thing you definitely don't need to  do is hoard all the toilet paper but  that's a story for another what if  [Music]  you

